fdisk's help names a flag the binary does not take.

The flag list says -T PARTTYPE, but getopt32 is given "t:" and the synopsis
already says [-t PARTTYPE]: only -t parses, -T is "unrecognized option". A
learner reading `fdisk --help` on the stick is taught the one spelling that
fails. This makes the flag list agree with the synopsis and the parser.
--- a/util-linux/fdisk.c
+++ b/util-linux/fdisk.c
@@ -104,7 +104,7 @@
 //but in fact, util-linux 2.41.1 shows the size in KILOBYTES!
 //usage:	)
 //usage:     "\n	-b 2048		(for certain MO disks) use 2048-byte sectors"
-//usage:     "\n	-T PARTTYPE	Force 'dos' partition if 'gpt' also present"
+//usage:     "\n	-t PARTTYPE	Force 'dos' partition if 'gpt' also present"
 //usage:     "\n	-C CYLINDERS	Set number of cylinders/heads/sectors"
 //usage:     "\n	-H HEADS	Typically 255"
 //usage:     "\n	-S SECTORS	Typically 63"
